{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "FaceCraft4D addresses the challenge of reconstructing a complete 3D dynamic model from a single 2D image, which is an inherently ill-posed problem due to the lack of depth, multiple viewpoints, and temporal information essential for accurate and consistent avatar generation [S3]. It also tackles the limitations of existing methods that require extensive multiview data or struggle with identity consistency and expression animation [S2]. The method overcomes these issues by leveraging shape, image, and video priors to generate multiview consistent avatars with realistic expressions [S4].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "Reconstructing a 3D dynamic model from a single 2D image is an inherently ill-posed problem, as critical 3D and temporal information is missing."},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "existing methods often require extensive inputs, such as multiview video with camera pose estimation, which is impractical for widespread use"},
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "we make use of 3 different types of priors: a shape prior, an image prior, and a video prior to synthesize personalized multiview images"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "AniPortrait's two main modules are Audio2Lmk and Lmk2Video. The Audio2Lmk module extracts a sequence of 3D facial mesh and head pose from audio input and projects them into a sequence of 2D facial landmarks [S1]. The Lmk2Video module takes the 2D facial landmark sequence and generates a temporally consistent and photorealistic portrait animation [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "The proposed framework comprises two modules, namely Audio2Lmk and Lmk2Video. The former is designed to extract a sequence of landmarks that captures intricate facial expressions and lip movements from audio input. The latter utilizes this landmark sequence to generate high-quality portrait videos with temporal stability."},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "In the second stage, we utilize a robust diffusion model... to transform the facial landmark sequence into a temporally consistent and photorealistic animated portrait."}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered", "answer": "The method separates driver identity from the avatar's appearance by using inter-frame feature differencing to extract only the time-varying driver-specific motion dynamics, effectively canceling out temporally stable target appearance features [S3]. This differencing operation removes appearance-related components by decomposing features into stable and dynamic parts, leaving only the motion-sensitive component that reflects the driving identity [S5]. The approach is preprocessing-free and operates directly on raw frames, avoiding reliance on facial landmarks that are tied to a fixed geometric vocabulary [S3]. The resulting representation is appearance-agnostic and driven solely by temporal facial micro-motion [S4].", "citations": [{"source_id": "S3", "quote": "Our method combines a micro-expression-aware F5C backbone with inter-frame feature differencing to capture fine-grained facial micro-motion while suppressing appearance structurally"},
{"source_id": "S5", "quote": "A central representation step... computes differences between consecutive feature maps... leaving in the feature signal only the time-varying component µt+1 -µt that encodes driver-specific motion"},
{"source_id": "S4", "quote": "The model must therefore learn an appearance-agnostic, driver-sensitive representation directly from raw frames"}], "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "E3 Gen makes 3D Gaussians usable for generative avatar modeling across subjects by proposing a generative UV features plane representation that encodes unstructured 3D Gaussians into a structured 2D UV space defined by the SMPL-X parametric model [S1]. This representation introduces a shared structure among subjects, enabling generative learning of diffusion models and facilitating cross-subject consistency [S2]. By leveraging the semantic structure of SMPL-X, the method ensures semantic consistency and allows for attribute transfer between subjects [S6]. The shared UV space supports disentangled geometry and appearance, enabling training with multiple subjects of varied body shapes [S3]. This structured representation overcomes the unstructured nature of 3D Gaussians, making them compatible with generative pipelines [S7].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "the proposed method introduces a generative UV features representation that encodes unstructured 3D Gaussians onto a structured 2D UV space defined by the SMPLX parametric model"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "this novel representation not only preserves the representation ability of the original 3D Gaussian but also introduces a shared structure among subjects to enable generative learning of the diffusion model"},
    {"source_id": "S6", "quote": "Benefiting from the shared structure among subjects, we can easily transfer geometry and appearance attributes from one generated subject to another by swapping the corresponding features"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "we further enhance the part-aware deformation module to allow for the disentanglement of body shape factors. This disentanglement enables training with multiple subjects possessing varied body shapes"},
    {"source_id": "S7", "quote": "our key insight is to project the unstructured 3D Gaussian into a structured 2D space. Following this idea, we propose a novel avatar representation, i.e., generative UV features plane"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "AVAPrintDB is intended to let researchers evaluate avatar fingerprinting systems under controlled and reproducible conditions across multiple state-of-the-art synthesis pipelines and source datasets [S1]. It enables the assessment of whether current systems can detect identity-related motion cues or rely on generator- or dataset-specific artifacts [S2]. The database supports evaluation of performance under generator and dataset shift, simulating both legitimate usage and impersonation scenarios [S1]. It facilitates the study of identity persistence in synthetic avatars and the robustness of avatar fingerprinting methods [S4].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "the task of avatar fingerprinting aims to determine whether two avatar videos are driven by the same human operator or not"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "This makes it possible to study whether current avatar fingerprinting systems truly capture identity-related behavioral cues or instead rely on generator-or dataset-specific artifacts"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "AVAPrintDB is constructed from two audiovisual corpora and three state-of-the-art avatar generators... including both self- and cross-reenactments to simulate legitimate usage and impersonation scenarios"},
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "we define a standardized and reproducible benchmark for avatar fingerprinting, including identity-disjoint splits, predefined verification trials, and evaluation protocols"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "our results show that, while identity-related motion cues persist across synthetic avatars, current avatar fingerprinting systems remain highly sensitive to changes in the synthesis pipeline and source domain"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "AniPortrait enhanced the pose guider module by adopting a multi-scale strategy inspired by ControlNet, incorporating landmark features at different scales into various blocks of the backbone network. This design improves the model's ability to capture intricate lip movements. Additionally, it introduces the reference image's landmark as an extra input, enabling cross-attention between reference and target landmarks to better align facial motion with appearance. These modifications ensure more precise lip motion reproduction compared to the original AnimateAnyone design, which used only a simple convolution layer before merging features [S2].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we adopt ControlNet's multi-scale strategy, incorporating landmark features of corresponding scales into different blocks of the backbone"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we introduce an additional improvement: the inclusion of the reference image's landmark as an extra input. The PoseGuider's cross-attention module facilitates interaction between the reference landmarks and each frame's target landmarks"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we discover that this rudimentary design falls short in capturing the intricate movements of the lips"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "E3 Gen can transfer face or clothing attributes from one generated avatar to another by leveraging the shared UV structure in the generative UV features plane, enabling attribute transfer between subjects [S1]. This is achieved by swapping the corresponding features associated with specific regions such as the face or clothing [S4]. The semantic consistency ensured by UV space facilitates the transfer of specific attributes like facial shape and clothing [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "Benefiting from the shared structure among subjects, we can easily transfer geometry and appearance attributes from one generated subject to another by swapping the corresponding features."},
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "Due to the shared UV structure provided by the UV features plane among subjects, attribute transfer between subjects can be easily performed."},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "The utilization of UV space, which ensures semantic consistency, further facilitates the transfer of specific regions such as the nose, clothing, and face."}
 ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "Avatar Forcing learns more expressive listening reactions through direct preference optimization (DPO) without human preference labels. It synthesizes underexpressive motion latents by dropping user signals and uses them as less-preferred samples, creating a clear preference signal for expressive motion [S3]. This method enables label-free learning of interactive behaviors by leveraging the contrast between expressive and underexpressive motion responses [S6]. The DPO framework aligns the model with human preferences implicitly by optimizing the preference between preferred and less-preferred samples [S5]. The resulting motion shows natural head movement and reactive expressions, such as smiling in response to user cues [S4].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "By synthesizing underexpressive motion latents via dropping the user signals and using them as less-preferred samples, we achieve significantly improved expressiveness and reactiveness, without the need for additional human annotations for natural interaction."},
    {"source_id": "S6", "quote": "We adapted a reward-free method, Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) [46, 58], for fine-tuning our motion generation based on diffusion forcing."},
    {"source_id": "S5", "quote": "Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) [46] aligns a model with human preferences without explicitly training a reward model."},
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "Our fine-tuned model generates an expressive avatar that shows natural head movement (Fig. 8 red box) and smiles more broadly along with the user (Fig. 8 red arrow)."},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "We propose a preference optimization method to enhance expressive interaction without the need for additional human labels."}
],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "Avatar Forcing adds look-ahead frames to its blockwise causal attention mask to mitigate severe per-frame motion jittering and ensure smooth temporal transitions across blocks, while preserving overall causality [S1]. The look-ahead enables each block to attend to a limited number of future frames, improving temporal consistency in generated motion [S2]. This design significantly enhances the temporal smoothness of the avatar motion compared to a simple blockwise causal structure [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "we introduce look-ahead in the causal mask, which allows each block to attend to a limited number of future frames while preserving overall causality."},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we introduce the Blockwise Casual Look-ahead Mask to ensure the causal motion generation in our motion latent space, which significantly improves the temporal consistency of the generated video"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "With look-ahead, we effectively mitigate severe per-frame motion jittering observed in the simple blockwise causal structure"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "If the model is not given the user's motion as input, it produces static behavior even when the user smiles or speaks, failing to react to non-verbal cues such as smiling or nodding [S1]. The avatar does not exhibit reactive or expressive motion, as shown in Fig. 7, where the model without user motion remains static despite strong non-verbal cues [S1]. This results in a lack of natural interaction and reduced expressiveness in motion [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "removing user motion leads to significantly less reactive behavior (Reactiveness) and reduced expressiveness in motion (Motion Richness)"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "the model without user motion produces static behavior whenever the user's audio is silent"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "the model cannot perceive visual signals"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "GaussianAvatar handles inaccurate body poses by jointly optimizing both motions and appearances through a differentiable motion condition. It refines the estimated body poses and translations (∆θ, ∆t) during the avatar modeling process using a forward skinning process, which improves both motion accuracy and appearance quality [S6]. The method enables joint optimization of motions and appearances, allowing the network to correct misalignments in initial motion capture results [S5]. By using an optimizable feature tensor and dynamic appearance network, it learns the motion-to-appearance mapping while refining the motion in real-time [S3]. This approach overcomes the limitations of previous methods that used indirect inverse skinning for pose optimization [S2].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S6", "quote": "we propose to jointly optimize human motions and appearances... using a forward skinning process"},
    {"source_id": "S5", "quote": "our network to refine the motion along with the avatar modeling process, which helps to tackle the long-standing issue of inaccurate motion estimation"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "a dynamic appearance network along with an optimizable feature tensor is designed to learn the motion-to-appearance mapping"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "attempting to address the inaccurate pose estimation issue with implicit human NeRF models has proven to be inefficient and imprecise"},
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "our method enables a joint optimization of motion and appearances during avatar modeling, which helps to tackle the long-standing issue of inaccurate motion estimation in monocular settings"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "When the F5C backbone is swapped for ResNet18 while keeping feature-space differencing, the performance remains well below that of F5C, indicating that a generic appearance-oriented encoder is poorly suited to feature-space differencing and cannot effectively capture fine-grained facial micro-motion [S2][S3]. This confirms that effective avatar fingerprinting depends on a backbone explicitly designed for micro-motion encoding [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "the best ResNet18 variant remains far below F5C despite using substantially more parameters"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "this setup allows us to test whether a generic appearance-oriented backbone can benefit from the same differencing strategy as F5C, or whether the gains depend on an encoder explicitly designed for fine-grained facial micro-motion"},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "relying on a generic appearance-oriented encoder does not preserve useful inter-frame variation"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "The main open limitations of the inter-frame feature differencing method include lack of evaluation on newer diffusion-based portrait generators and subjects with weak facial expressivity [S2]. The method also does not study robustness against adaptive or adversarial attacks that may suppress temporal cues [S2]. A more formal disentanglement analysis is needed to strengthen the theoretical grounding of the approach [S2].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "Our evaluation is currently limited to NVFAIR, which covers three pre-diffusion reenactment generators... remains to be established."},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we do not study robustness against adaptive or adversarial attacks; an informed attacker may attempt to suppress or perturb the temporal cues exploited by inter-frame differencing."},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "a more formal disentanglement analysis would strengthen the theoretical grounding of the method."}
],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "partial",
 "answer": "MotionDiffuse enables body-part independent control using noise interpolation, allowing separate control of different body parts while considering their correlations [S4]. The multi-track timeline paper describes a similar technique by assigning text prompts to specific body parts at each timestep and stitching body-part motions together during test-time denoising [S2]. Both methods achieve spatial composition by decoupling motion generation per body part [S4][S2].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "we propose 'noise interpolation' to separately control different body parts while taking their correlations into consideration"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we first pre-process the input timeline to assign a text prompt to each body part at every timestep"}
  ],
 "caveats": ["The multi-track timeline paper does not explicitly describe noise interpolation, which is central to MotionDiffuse's method; it focuses on timeline-based stitching during denoising rather than the underlying noise mechanism [S2]."]}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "Increasing the latent dimension matters more than the number of transformer layers in MotionDiffuse. The ablation studies show that models with a 512 latent dimension significantly outperform those with 128 or 256 dimensions across multiple metrics, including FID and R Precision. While increasing the number of layers improves performance when the latent dimension is small (e.g., 128 or 256), it has little effect when the dimension is 512 [S1][S2].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "latent dimension is more important than the number of layers"},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "the latent dimension plays a more important role"}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "Real3D-Portrait achieves a higher lip synchronization score than PC-AVS in audio-driven talking face generation. Specifically, in the audio-driven setting, Real3D-Portrait achieves a Sync score of 6.565, while PC-AVS achieves a Sync score of 6.483, indicating better lip synchronization performance [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {
      "source_id": "S1",
      "quote": "Methods: PC-AVS; CSIM↑: 0.327; FID↓: 82.02; AED↓: 0.162; Sync↑: 6.483"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "S1",
      "quote": "Methods: Ours (AD); CSIM↑: 0.763; FID↓: 43.02; AED↓: 0.146; Sync↑: 6.565"
    }
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "S1 proposes adding watermarks to generated videos to mitigate risks of identity spoofing and malicious deepfakes [S1]. S2 advocates for watermarking or clearly annotating facial sequences as synthetic data to address misuse risks [S2]. S3 mentions inserting both visible and invisible video watermarks for generated content identification [S3]. S4 encourages watermarking as a common approach to prevent misuse [S4].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "Adding watermarks to generated videos and applying a restricted license can help mitigate these risks."},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "we strongly advocate for watermarking or clearly annotating facial sequences generated by our method as synthetic data."},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "Both visible and invisible video watermarks will be inserted into the produced video for generated content identification similar to Dall-E and Imagen."},
    {"source_id": "S4", "quote": "To prevent misuse, adding watermarks is a common approach."}
 ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "SadTalker produces varied, stylised head movements by using a conditional VAE, PoseVAE, which models the residual head pose relative to an initial pose. It learns the residual motion ∆ρ{1,...,t} = ρ{1,...,t} - ρ0 under conditions such as the first frame pose, style identity, and audio clip, enabling diverse and life-like head motions aligned with the audio [S6]. The system generates stylized head poses by conditioning on style identity and audio, allowing for natural and varied head movements [S2].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S6", "quote": "We learn the residual of the input head pose ρ0 via a conditional VAE structure. Given the conditions: first frame ρ0, style identity Zstyle and the audio clip a{1,...,t}, our method learns a distribution of the residual head pose ∆ρ{1,...,t} = ρ{1,...,t} - ρ0."},
    {"source_id": "S2", "quote": "PoseVAE models head movements in diverse styles, ensuring coherence with the beat alignment to audio."}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "The SadTalker authors list two main limitations: (1) the lack of modeling for eye and tooth variations due to 3DMMs not capturing these details, which affects realism in teeth synthesis [S1]; and (2) the generated video has a fixed emotion, as the method only models lip motion and eye blinking, not other facial expressions like emotion or gaze direction, reducing overall realism [S1].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "Since 3DMMs do not model the variation of eyes and teeth, the mappingNet in our Face Render will also struggle to synthesize the realistic teeth in some cases."},
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "Another limitation of our work is that we only concern the lip motion and eye blinking other than the other facial expressions, e.g., emotion and gaze direction. Thus, the generated video has a fixed emotion, which also reduces the realism of generated content."}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

{"status": "answered",
 "answer": "MDM-SMPL represents poses with SMPL joint rotations to avoid expensive test-time optimization and to enable body-part stitching due to the propagation of changes through the kinematic tree [S1]. This representation allows direct SMPL output and significantly reduces the number of diffusion steps from 1000 to 100, resulting in a 10× speed-up in sampling [S3].",
 "citations": [
    {"source_id": "S1", "quote": "this SMPL-based representation avoids the need for expensive test-time optimization to fit the generated motion on a SMPL body"},
    {"source_id": "S3", "quote": "fewer steps, combined with pre-computing text embeddings, enable sampling MDM-SMPL in less than 5 seconds on average. This is a substantial improvement over MDM, which takes 4 minutes to generate motions followed by 8 min of optimization to obtain SMPL poses, on average."}
  ],
 "caveats": []}

